configuration
  ✓ reverse proxy: deploy/Caddyfile.local serves rec03.video.test, which matches PUBLIC_BASE_URL
  ⚠ domain DNS: skipped: VIDRA_TLS_MODE=internal: certificates are issued by Caddy's own local CA, so no ACME order depends on this host's DNS
  ⚠ env file vs template: env/production.env.example defines 1 key(s) env/production.env does not assign: CLAMAV_ADDR
      → re-run `vidra setup --template env/production.env.example --output env/production.env --yes` to fill them in — it preserves every value the file already sets, including the KEKs, and adds the ones a newer template introduced
  ⚠ env file vs template: env/production.env assigns 2 key(s) env/production.env.example does not define: API_ROLE, EXTRA_COMPOSE_PROFILES
      → check each one is still read by something. A key the template dropped is usually harmless (it is simply unused), but a MISSPELLED one looks exactly the same from here and takes its setting with it
  ⚠ configuration values: VIDRA_TLS_MODE=internal does not produce a publicly trusted certificate: internal is a rehearsal/bring-up mode, and every browser reaching this instance will refuse the connection. Set VIDRA_TLS_MODE=acme in env/production.env and regenerate the Caddyfile once DNS points at this host

data & state
  ✓ schema ledger: the core migration ledger (schema_migrations) is at version 146 and clean
  ⚠ search ledger: skipped: this deployment uses the bundled Postgres, which publishes no host port, and the api image's `migrate version` only reports core's ledger
  ✓ db pool sizing: the connection pool fits the server: each process opens up to 10 connections (DB_MAX_CONNS) and this PostgreSQL accepts 100, of which 3 are reserved for superusers — so 97 usable. Total demand is DB_MAX_CONNS × PROCESSES: 20 for the stock api+worker split (2), and more for every extra replica
  ✓ backups: the last successful backup was 2m18s ago (2026-09-13T05:13:41Z)
  ✓ backup timer: vidra-backup.timer is enabled and active (daily at 03:15 with jitter)
  ✓ disk space: the checkout (and backups/): 300.6 GiB free of 308.9 GiB (97%)
  ✓ media GC posture: media garbage collection runs daily and deletes stored objects no database row references; a sweep that finds more than 25% of what it scanned to be orphans deletes nothing instead (MEDIA_GC_MAX_ORPHAN_PERCENT). The first sweep after each restart is always a dry run
  ✓ storage migration: no storage migration in flight — media is served from, and garbage-collected in, one store
  ⚠ cdn purge backlog: skipped: this deployment uses the bundled Postgres, which publishes no host port (by design) — read the same numbers in the cdn_purge block of GET /api/v1/admin/system
  ✓ instance owner: the instance owner is marked and protected from other administrators — though it is the only active admin, so the last-admin guard is what is holding the console open: promote a second admin before that account goes anywhere

reachability
  ✓ object storage: STORAGE_BACKEND=local — media lives in the media_data volume, so there is no object store to reach (make sure that volume is in your backups)
  ✓ object write: STORAGE_BACKEND=local — media lives in the media_data volume, so there is no object-store credential that could turn out to be read-only
  ✓ object retention: media lives on local disk, where a delete frees the bytes immediately — object versioning does not apply
  ✓ bucket ownership: media lives on local disk, which this instance populated itself — there is no shared bucket to mistake for its own, so the ownership marker does not apply
  ✓ smtp: mail is off (MAIL_ENABLED is not true, or SMTP_HOST is blank) — password reset and email verification are unavailable, which is a deliberate configuration and not a fault
  ✓ search service: the search service answers /healthz inside the compose network (Up 40 seconds (healthy))
  ✓ ffmpeg: ffmpeg is present in the api container: ffmpeg version 8.1.2 Copyright (c) 2000-2026 the FFmpeg developers
  ✓ video encoders: only H.264 is enabled, which every client can play and every ffmpeg can encode — there is no extra encoder to check
  ✓ hardware transcode: hardware transcode available: vaapi (/dev/dri/renderD128) — enable it with TRANSCODING_HW=vaapi. CPU encoding is the default and always works; hardware is faster and cheaper but depends on this host keeping that device (checked against the api container's ffmpeg)

